<chapter xml:id="chp-workshop-timeline">
  <title>Workshop Timeline</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This chapter is the spine of the workshop. Each section below corresponds to one phase of the afternoon, in order. The action phases — <em>Guided Practice</em> and <em>Build a Lesson</em> — are self-contained: everything you need to follow along lives inside that section. The shorter talk and transition phases are lighter, and point you to the slides or to other chapters of this hub when useful.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you fall behind or want to jump ahead, use the table of contents on the left. Nothing here depends on you having finished the previous phase perfectly.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- 4:00-4:10 Welcome                                  -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-welcome">
    <title>4:00-4:10 Welcome</title>

    <p>
      Thanks for coming. In the next two hours, you will author, preview, and publish a small lesson using <pretext/>, entirely in your web browser. No installs, no command line, no GitHub account required for today.
    </p>

    <p>
      We'll start with a short orientation, take a quick tour of what <pretext/> can do, then spend most of our time in a hands-on editor.
    </p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Slides and reference material</title>
      <p>
        <ul marker="">
          <li><url href="https://geoff-cox.github.io/maa26-pretext-workshop/talk/slides.html">Slides</url></li>
          <li><url href="https://runestone.academy/ns/books/published/debookrs/">Sample textbook (viewable HTML)</url></li>
          <li><url href="https://github.com/geoff-cox/debookrs">Sample textbook (source on GitHub)</url></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>What you'll leave with</title>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>A working <pretext/> authoring setup in your browser that you can return to tomorrow.</li>
          <li>A small lesson you wrote yourself, on a topic you actually teach.</li>
          <li>A shareable link to that lesson.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- 4:10-4:20 What Can PreTeXt Do?                     -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-book-tour">
    <title>4:10-4:20 What Can <pretext/> Do?</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Before you write any <pretext/> yourself, it helps to see where this is going. The key idea is that you describe <em>what your content is</em> — a definition, an example, an exercise, an interactive — and <pretext/> decides how it should look on the page, in a PDF, on a phone, or through a screen reader.
      </p>

      <p>
        The gallery below shows some of what's possible. You are <em>not</em> expected to build any of this today. Skim it, get a sense of the range, and come back to it later when you want ideas.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="talk-demo-interactive-features">
      <title>Interactive Feature Gallery</title>

      <subsubsection xml:id="talk-demo-geogebra">
        <title>GeoGebra</title>
        <p>
          GeoGebra is a powerful tool for creating interactive math visualizations. You can build an applet in GeoGebra and then embed it directly into a <pretext/> document with a single tag.
        </p>

        <interactive geogebra="KGn2d4Qd" width="100%" aspect="3:1" reset-icon="yes"/>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="talk-demo-desmos">
        <title>Desmos</title>
        <p>
          Desmos is another popular tool for creating interactive math visualizations. Like GeoGebra, Desmos graphs embed directly into a <pretext/> document.
        </p>
        <interactive desmos="ttox1bvxku" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="talk-demo-google-maps">
        <title>Google Maps</title>
        <p>
          Anything with an embed code will work, not just math tools. Here is a Google Map embedded the same way.
        </p>
        <interactive iframe="https://www.google.com/maps/embed?pb=!1m18!1m12!1m3!1d3692.286430663061!2d-77.02612656602201!3d38.90499299644047!2m3!1f0!2f0!3f0!3m2!1i1024!2i768!4f13.1!3m3!1m2!1s0x89b7b7ed3715b137%3A0x32771de147fa5350!2sWalter%20E.%20Washington%20Convention%20Center!5e0!3m2!1sen!2sus!4v1766949594608!5m2!1sen!2sus" width="95%" aspect="2:1"/>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="talk-demo-true-false">
        <title>✔ True/False Questions</title>

        <p>
          Auto-graded true/false questions with targeted feedback — students see an explanation tailored to the answer they gave.
        </p>

        <exercise label="vector-space-dimension">
          <title>True/False</title>
          <statement correct="no">
            <p>
              Every vector space has finite dimension.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The vector space of all polynomials with finite degree has a basis,
              <m>B = \{1,x,x^2,x^3,\dots\}</m>, which is infinite.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </exercise>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="talk-demo-multiple-choice">
        <title>✔ Multiple-Choice Questions</title>

        <p>
          Multiple choice with per-option feedback, hints that reveal in sequence, and the option to randomize choices at build time.
        </p>

        <exercise label="multiple-choice-not-randomized">
          <title>Multiple-Choice, Not Randomized, One Answer</title>
          <statement>
            <p>What color is a stop sign?</p>
          </statement>
          <choices>
            <choice>
              <statement>
                <p>Green</p>
              </statement>
              <feedback>
                <p>Green means<q>go!</q>.</p>
              </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
              <statement>
                <p>Red</p>
              </statement>
              <feedback>
                <p>Red is universally used for prohibited activities or serious warnings.</p>
              </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
              <statement>
                <p>White</p>
              </statement>
              <feedback>
                <p>White might be hard to see.</p>
              </feedback>
            </choice>
          </choices>
          <hint><p>What did you see last time you went driving?</p></hint>
          <hint><p>Maybe go out for a drive?</p></hint>
        </exercise>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="talk-demo-matching">
        <title>✔ Matching Questions</title>

        <p>
          Drag-and-drop matching, useful for pre-reading checks or review.
        </p>

        <exercise label="lt-common-transforms-prereading-questions-1"><title>Exponential Integration Rules</title>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Match each function of <m>t</m> with its antiderivative. Assume <m>K</m> is a constant.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <cardsort>
            <match>
              <premise><me>f(t) = \frac{K}{t}</me></premise>
              <response><me>K\ln|t| + C</me></response>
            </match>
            <match>
              <premise><me>g(t) = \frac{1}{K}</me></premise>
              <response><me>\frac{t}{K} + C</me></response>
            </match>
            <match>
              <premise><me>h(t) = e^{Kt}</me></premise>
              <response><me>\frac{1}{K} e^{Kt} + C</me></response>
            </match>
            <match>
              <premise><me>h(t) = e^{K}</me></premise>
              <response><me>t e^{K} + C</me></response>
            </match>
            <match>
              <premise><me>m(t) = \frac{t}{K}</me></premise>
              <response><me>\frac{t^2}{2K} + C</me></response>
            </match>
            <match>
              <response><me>\ln|K| + C</me></response>
            </match>
          </cardsort>
        </exercise>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="talk-demo-clickable-area">
        <title>✔ Clickable-Area Questions</title>

        <p>
          Let students pick out the correct formula, diagram, or expression by clicking on it directly.
        </p>

        <exercise xml:id="lt-derivative-transfer-prereading-questions-2">
          <title><e component="emoji">📖❓</e>  Integration by Parts</title>
          <statement>
            <p>Click on the correct integration by parts formula.</p>
          </statement>
          <areas>
            <p><m>\vphantom{m}</m></p>
            <sidebyside margins="10% 5%">
              <p><area correct="no"> <m>\displaystyle \int u\ dv = u\ v + \int v\ du</m></area></p>
              <p><area correct="no"> <m>\displaystyle \int u\ dv = u\ v - \int u\ dv</m></area></p>
            </sidebyside>
            <p><m>\vphantom{m}</m></p>
            <sidebyside margins="10% 5%">
              <p><area correct="yes"><m>\displaystyle \int u\ dv = u\ v - \int v\ du</m></area></p>
              <p><area correct="no"> <m>\displaystyle \int u\ dv = u\ dv - \int v\ du</m></area></p>
            </sidebyside>
            <p><m>\vphantom{m}</m></p>
          </areas>
        </exercise>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="talk-demo-fill-in-blank">
        <title>✔ Fill-in-the-Blank Questions</title>

        <p>
          Accept any answer in a given set, or write your own check with a small snippet of JavaScript for richer feedback.
        </p>

        <exercise label="ex-demo-jscmp-primes">
        <title>Fill-In, Prime less then 20</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            What is an example of a prime number less than 20?
            <fillin width="5" answer="13"/>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
          <evaluate>
            <test correct="yes">
              <jscmp><!-- test if number is in a list -->
                [2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19].includes(Number(ans))
              </jscmp>
              <feedback>
                <p>
                  Any number from the list <m>\{2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19\}</m> is a prime number less than 20.
                </p>
              </feedback>
            </test>
            <test>
              <jscmp><!-- find why it fails to be in list -->
                function(){
                  const val=Number(ans);
                  if (val &lt;= 0) { return "You need to give a positive integer." }
                  if (val &gt;= 20) { return "The integer must be less than 20." }
                  for (let i=2; i&lt;=3; i++) {
                  if (val % i == 0) { return `Your answer is composite since it is divisible by ${i}.` }
                }
                return false;
                }()
              </jscmp>
            </test>
          </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        </exercise>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="talk-demo-coding">
        <title>✔ Interactive Coding Questions</title>

        <p>
          Live Python (and several other languages) right inside the page. Students run and modify the code without leaving the textbook.
        </p>

        <program label="partial-program-two" interactive="activecode" language="python">
          <code>
            num = 569
            nDigits = 0
            print(f"How many digits are in the integer {num}")
            temp = num
            while temp > 1:
              temp = temp/10
              nDigits = nDigits + 1
              print(f"  temp={temp}")

            print(f"{num} has {nDigits} digits")
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          If your book is hosted on the Runestone server, you can also attach graded questions to the code. See <url href="https://runestone.academy/ns/books/published/PTXSB/code-lens.html#program-codelens-python-questions">CodeLens with questions</url> for an example.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="talk-demo-datasets">
        <title>✔ Coding with Datasets</title>

        <p>
          You can import a dataset from a CSV or text file — or hardcode one into the source — and have students work with it inside the page.
        </p>

        <datafile label="file-global-climate-one" xml:id="file-global-climate-one" filename="ccdata1.txt" rows="18" cols="40" editable="yes" include-source="no">
          <pre>
          1850    -0.37     2.24E-7
          1860    -0.34     3.94E-7
          1870    -0.28     6.6E-7
          1880    -0.24     1.10
          1890    -0.42     1.72
          1900    -0.20     2.38
          1910    -0.49     3.34
          1920    -0.25     4.01
          1930    -0.14     4.53
          1940    +0.01     5.50
          1950    -0.17     6.63
          1960    -0.05     10.5
          1970    -0.03     16.0
          1980    +0.09     20.3
          1990    +0.30     22.6
          2000    +0.29     24.9
          2010    +0.56     32.7
          2019    +0.74     33.3
          </pre>
        </datafile>

        <p>
          Below, the data above is loaded and filtered by a Python program that students can run and edit in place.
        </p>

        <program label="python-average-climate" xml:id="python-average-climate" interactive="activecode" language="python" include-source="no">
          <code>
            ccfile = open("ccdata1.txt", "r")

            print("Years with increasing avg. temp")
            print("YEAR  △ TEMP(°C)  CO2 (gigatons)")
            for aline in ccfile:
              values = aline.split()
              if float(values[1]) > 0:
                print(f"{values[0]}  {values[1]}        {values[2]}")

            ccfile.close()
          </code>
        </program>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>DoenetML</title>
        <p>
          DoenetML is a newer platform designed specifically for interactive math documents. The example below lets students drag vectors to visualize a determinant identity — the kind of thing that is genuinely hard to show on paper.
        </p>

        <p>
          <interactive label="interactive-doenetml-example" platform="doenetml" width="100%">
            <slate surface="doenetml">
            &lt;p&gt; Adjust the vectors &lt;m&gt;\vec u&lt;/m&gt;, &lt;m&gt;\vec v&lt;/m&gt;, and &lt;m&gt;\vec w&lt;/m&gt; in the left graph to visualize the areas calculated by &lt;m&gt;\det[\vec u\hspace{0.5em}\vec w]&lt;/m&gt;, &lt;m&gt;\det[\vec v\hspace{0.5em}\vec w]&lt;/m&gt;, and &lt;m&gt;\det[\vec u+\vec v\hspace{0.5em}\vec w]&lt;/m&gt;. &lt;/p&gt;
            &lt;setup&gt;
            &lt;line through="(0,0) $v" name="vLine"/&gt;
            &lt;math simplify="true" name="c"&gt;$cv.x/$v.x&lt;/math&gt;
            &lt;/setup&gt;
            &lt;sideBySide&gt;
            &lt;graph xmin="-4" ymin="-4" xmax="16" ymax="16"&gt;
            &lt;m draggable="false" anchor="(10,14)"&gt;
            \det[\vec u\hspace{0.5em}\vec w],
            \det[\vec v\hspace{0.5em}\vec w]
            &lt;/m&gt;
            &lt;point name="u" styleNumber="3"&gt;
            (6,2)
            &lt;label&gt;&lt;m&gt;\vec u&lt;/m&gt;&lt;/label&gt;
            &lt;/point&gt;
            &lt;point name="w" styleNumber="3"&gt;
            (3,7)
            &lt;label&gt;&lt;m&gt;\vec w&lt;/m&gt;&lt;/label&gt;
            &lt;/point&gt;
            &lt;point name="uPlusV" styleNumber="4"&gt;
            (9,5)
            &lt;label&gt;&lt;m&gt;\vec v&lt;/m&gt;&lt;/label&gt;
            &lt;/point&gt;
            &lt;polygon
            vertices="(0,0) $u $u+$w $w"
            filled="true"
            draggable="false"
            styleNumber="3"
            /&gt;
            &lt;polygon
            vertices="$u $uPlusV $uPlusV+$w $u+$w"
            filled="true"
            draggable="false"
            styleNumber="4"
            /&gt;
            &lt;lineSegment endpoints="(0,0) $uPlusV" styleNumber="6"/&gt;
            &lt;lineSegment endpoints="$w $uPlusV+$w" styleNumber="6"/&gt;
            &lt;/graph&gt;
            &lt;graph xmin="-4" ymin="-4" xmax="16" ymax="16"&gt;
            &lt;m draggable="false" anchor="(12,14)"&gt;
            \det[\vec u+\vec v\hspace{0.5em}\vec w]
            &lt;/m&gt;
            &lt;polygon
            vertices="(0,0) $uPlusV $uPlusV+$w $w"
            filled="true"
            draggable="false"
            styleNumber="2"
            /&gt;
            &lt;point draggable="false" styleNumber="3"&gt;
            $w
            &lt;label&gt;&lt;m&gt;\vec w&lt;/m&gt;&lt;/label&gt;
            &lt;/point&gt;
            &lt;point draggable="false" styleNumber="4"&gt;
            $uPlusV
            &lt;label&gt;&lt;m&gt;\vec u+\vec v&lt;/m&gt;&lt;/label&gt;
            &lt;/point&gt;
            &lt;lineSegment endpoints="(0,0) $u" styleNumber="6"/&gt;
            &lt;lineSegment endpoints="$u $uPlusV" styleNumber="6"/&gt;
            &lt;lineSegment endpoints="$w $w+$u" styleNumber="6"/&gt;
            &lt;lineSegment endpoints="$w+$u $w+$uPlusV" styleNumber="6"/&gt;
            &lt;/graph&gt;
            &lt;/sideBySide&gt;
            </slate>
          </interactive>
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>JSXGraph</title>

        <p>
          JSXGraph is a lightweight JavaScript library for interactive geometry and function plots. <pretext/> lets you author JSXGraph code in a separate file and pull it in with a short tag.
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
          <interactive xml:id="interactive-fermats-spiral" platform="jsxgraph" source="code/fermats-spiral.js">
            <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-fermats-spiral" surface="jsxboard"/>
          </interactive>

          <interactive xml:id="interactive-bspline" platform="jsxgraph" source="code/bspline.js">
            <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-bspline" surface="jsxboard"/>
          </interactive>
        </sidebyside>

        <interactive xml:id="interactive-infinity" platform="jsxgraph" width="100%" source="code/infinity.js">
          <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-infinity" surface="jsxboard" aspect="1:1"/>
        </interactive>
      </subsubsection>

    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="textbook-gallery">
      <title>Textbook Gallery</title>

      <p>
        The features above are nice in isolation, but they're more convincing in context. The links below point at real textbook pages built with <pretext/>, each one showing off a different capability.
      </p>

      <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-textbook-gallery">
        <title>Things to try</title>
        <p>
          <ul marker="">
            <li>▷ <url href="https://runestone.academy/ns/books/published/debookrs/connection-to-alg-calc.html">Audio</url> — embedded narration.</li>
            <li>▷ <url href="https://runestone.academy/ns/books/published/debookrs/class-problems-main.html">Multi-part Questions</url> (see problem 2) — one exercise broken into scaffolded parts.</li>
            <li>▷ <url href="https://runestone.academy/ns/books/published/debookrs/visualizing-solutions.html">Interactive Visualization</url>.</li>
            <li>▷ <url href="https://runestone.academy/ns/books/published/debookrs/autonomous-eqns.html">TikZ</url> — <latex/> graphics rendered as accessible SVG.</li>
            <li>▷ <url href="https://runestone.academy/ns/books/published/debookrs/chpt-laplace-transform-method.html">Slide Show</url> — a lecture deck built from the same source.</li>
            <li>▷ <url href="https://runestone.academy/ns/books/published/debookrs/unit-step-functions.html#def-windowed-unit-step">More Visualization</url>.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </assemblage>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- 4:20-4:35 PreTeXt.Plus                             -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-pretextplus-tour">
    <title>4:20-4:35 PreTeXt.Plus</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        For today's workshop, everyone works inside <url href="https://pretext.plus"><c>pretext.plus</c></url> — a browser-based editor for <pretext/> that needs nothing installed. You'll make a free account, create a project, and get oriented to the editor. By the end of this phase you should have an empty project open and be ready to paste in content.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="pp-create-account">
      <title>Create your account</title>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Go to <url href="https://pretext.plus"><c>pretext.plus</c></url> and click <term>Sign up</term>. Use an email you can check right now — there's a confirmation step.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Confirm your email, then log in.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="pp-first-project">
      <title>Create your first project</title>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Click <term>Projects</term> in the top nav, then the blue <term>New project</term> button.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Give it a title — <c>My First Lesson</c> works fine — and click <term>Create</term>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Click the gray <term>Edit</term> button on your new project. The editor opens with <pretext/> source on the left and a live preview on the right.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="pp-rebuild-rhythm">
      <title>Learn the rebuild rhythm</title>

      <p>
        The preview does <em>not</em> update automatically. After every edit, you need to rebuild.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>Click the green <term>Rebuild</term> button at the top right of the preview, <em>or</em></li>
          <li>Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd> (this also saves your work).</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        <alert>The rhythm is: Edit → Rebuild → look at the preview.</alert> You'll repeat that cycle dozens of times this afternoon. It becomes automatic within about ten minutes.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="pp-orientation">
      <title>A quick orientation</title>

      <p>
        Before we start editing, take thirty seconds to notice:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>The <em>source panel</em> on the left is where you type. It's <pretext/> XML — we'll get to what that means in the next section.</li>
          <li>The <em>preview panel</em> on the right is what your readers will see.</li>
          <li>There's a short starter document already loaded. Don't delete it — you'll paste new material <em>inside</em> it.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        You're ready. On to the exercises.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- 4:35-5:00 Guided Practice                          -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-guided-practice">
    <title>4:35-5:00 Guided Practice</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Five short exercises. Each one shows you a single <pretext/> structure, you paste it into your project, rebuild, and see the result. By the end, you'll recognize most of the building blocks a typical lesson uses.
      </p>

      <p>
        <alert>How to work each exercise:</alert> find the closing <c>&lt;/section&gt;</c> tag near the bottom of your source and paste the snippet on a new line <em>just above it</em>. Then rebuild.
      </p>

      <p>
        <em>Already know <latex/>?</em> Most of the math inside the tags is still <latex/> — you're mostly learning a new wrapper, not a new language.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="gp-exercise-1">
      <title>Exercise 1 — A paragraph with inline math</title>

      <p>
        Paste this inside the section and rebuild. Notice how inline math flows with the text, the same way <c>\(...\)</c> does in <latex/>.
      </p>

      <program language="xml">
        <code><![CDATA[<p>
  The Pythagorean theorem states that <m>a^2 + b^2 = c^2</m>,
  where <m>c</m> is the length of the hypotenuse.
</p>]]></code>
      </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="gp-exercise-2">
      <title>Exercise 2 — A definition</title>

      <p>
        Add a formal definition. The <c>xml:id</c> attribute is a label we'll use in Exercise 5 to link to this definition from elsewhere in the document.
      </p>

      <program language="xml">
        <code><![CDATA[<definition xml:id="def-right-triangle">
  <statement>
    <p>
      A <term>right triangle</term> is a triangle
      with one angle measuring exactly 90 degrees.
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>]]></code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Notice that <pretext/> numbered the definition automatically and styled it as a named block. You didn't have to ask.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="gp-exercise-3">
      <title>Exercise 3 — An example</title>

      <p>
        Examples get numbered automatically too, in their own sequence. Try changing the title after you rebuild to see what happens.
      </p>

      <program language="xml">
        <code><![CDATA[<example>
  <title>A 3-4-5 triangle</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Show that a triangle with sides 3, 4, and 5
      is a right triangle.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      Check: <m>3^2 + 4^2 = 9 + 16 = 25 = 5^2</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>]]></code>
      </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="gp-exercise-4">
      <title>Exercise 4 — An exercise</title>

      <p>
        Exercises are numbered separately from examples — you can see the two sequences in your preview.
      </p>

      <program language="xml">
        <code><![CDATA[<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the length of the hypotenuse of a right
      triangle with legs of length 5 and 12.
    </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>]]></code>
      </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="gp-exercise-5">
      <title>Exercise 5 — A cross-reference</title>

      <p>
        This is the payoff. The <c>&lt;xref&gt;</c> tag below points at the <c>xml:id</c> you gave the definition in Exercise 2, and <pretext/> builds a working link for you — with the right number, the right wording, and the right target. Change the definition's position in the document and the link still works.
      </p>

      <program language="xml">
        <code><![CDATA[<p>
  For a reminder of the terminology,
  see <xref ref="def-right-triangle"/>.
</p>]]></code>
      </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="gp-finished-early">
      <title>Finished early?</title>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>Change the title of your example.</li>
          <li>Swap the 3-4-5 example for a 5-12-13 triangle.</li>
          <li>Break something on purpose — forget a closing tag, misspell <c>statement</c> — and see what the error looks like. You'll recognize it faster next time.</li>
          <li>Add a second <c>&lt;xref&gt;</c> pointing to your example, using the same pattern.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- 5:00-5:05 What's Next?                             -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-whats-next">
    <title>5:00-5:05 What's Next?</title>

    <p>
      Quick pause to set up the back half of the workshop. After the break you'll leave these small exercises behind and build a lesson of your own — still in the same <c>pretext.plus</c> project, just filling it in with your own material.
    </p>

    <p>
      You'll have about twenty-five minutes to write, five minutes to share with a neighbor, and then we'll look at a handful of lessons together before closing out with Q&amp;A and a quick look at what's next if you want to keep going after today.
    </p>

    <p>
      Before you stand up, <em>save your project</em> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd>). Everything lives in your account — you can close the tab and come back.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- 5:05-5:10 Break                                    -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-break">
    <title>5:05-5:10 Break</title>

    <p>
      Take five. Stretch, refill water, say hi to a neighbor. We'll start again at 5:10 sharp.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- 5:10-5:35 Build a Lesson                           -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-build-lesson">
    <title>5:10-5:35 Build a Lesson</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This is the main event. You have about twenty-five minutes to build a short lesson using the structures you met in Guided Practice. Pick an approach below based on how confident you're feeling and what you want to work on.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="bl-choose-your-path">
      <title>Choose your path</title>

      <p>
        <dl>
          <li>
            <title>Path A — Use a starter</title>
            <p>
              Pick one of the two starter lessons below, paste it into your project, rebuild, and then <em>make it yours</em> — change the topic, swap the example, rewrite the exercise. The scaffolding is already there; you're customizing.
            </p>
            <p>
              Best if you want a guaranteed win or you aren't sure what to write about.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Path B — Write your own</title>
            <p>
              Start from a topic you actually teach and build a short lesson from scratch using the patterns from Guided Practice. Aim for: one paragraph of motivation, one definition, one worked example, one exercise, and one cross-reference.
            </p>
            <p>
              Best if you already have something in mind and want to try the real thing.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        Either path is a success. Nobody is getting graded on ambition.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="bl-starter-triangles">
      <title>Starter option 1 — Right triangles</title>

      <p>
        A geometry lesson with a definition, a named example, and an exercise that references the definition by cross-reference.
      </p>

      <program language="xml">
        <code><![CDATA[<title>Right Triangles</title>

<p>
  This short lesson introduces right triangles and
  the Pythagorean theorem.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-right-triangle">
  <statement>
    <p>
      A <term>right triangle</term> is a triangle
      with one angle measuring exactly 90 degrees.
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<p>
  The Pythagorean theorem states that
  <m>a^2 + b^2 = c^2</m>, where <m>c</m> is the
  length of the hypotenuse.
</p>

<example>
  <title>A 3-4-5 triangle</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Show that a triangle with sides 3, 4, and 5
      is a right triangle.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      Check: <m>3^2 + 4^2 = 9 + 16 = 25 = 5^2</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the length of the hypotenuse of a right
      triangle with legs of length 5 and 12.
    </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>

<p>
  For a reminder of the terminology,
  see <xref ref="def-right-triangle"/>.
</p>]]></code>
      </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="bl-starter-product-rule">
      <title>Starter option 2 — The product rule</title>

      <p>
        A calculus lesson with a display-math definition, a worked example that uses the definition, and an exercise that sends students back to the definition via cross-reference.
      </p>

      <program language="xml">
        <code><![CDATA[<title>The Product and Quotient Rules</title>

<p>
  When a function is built by multiplying or dividing
  two simpler functions, we cannot just multiply or
  divide their derivatives.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-product-rule">
  <statement>
    <p>
      The <term>product rule</term> states that if
      <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are differentiable, then
      <me>
        \frac{d}{dx}\bigl[f(x)\,g(x)\bigr]
        = f'(x)\,g(x) + f(x)\,g'(x).
      </me>
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<example>
  <title>Applying the product rule</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the derivative of <m>h(x) = x^2 \sin(x)</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      Let <m>f(x) = x^2</m> and <m>g(x) = \sin(x)</m>.
      Then
      <me>
        h'(x) = 2x \sin(x) + x^2 \cos(x).
      </me>
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Use the product rule
      (see <xref ref="def-product-rule"/>) to find
      the derivative of <m>p(x) = x^3 e^x</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>]]></code>
      </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="bl-milestones">
      <title>Milestones</title>

      <p>
        Whichever path you pick, aim for these milestones. Don't worry about hitting them in order.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <em>A title and a paragraph.</em> The lesson has a topic and a short lead-in.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <em>A definition with an <c>xml:id</c>.</em> Your reader has a formal statement of the main idea.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <em>A worked example.</em> You show the reader how the idea gets used.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <em>An exercise.</em> The reader gets a chance to practice.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <em>A cross-reference.</em> Somewhere in the lesson, an <c>&lt;xref/&gt;</c> links back to your definition or example.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        Hitting all five in twenty-five minutes is very doable. Hitting three is also fine. You're building a habit, not a textbook.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="bl-if-stuck">
      <title>If you get stuck</title>

      <p>
        <dl>
          <li>
            <title>Red error in the preview</title>
            <p>
              Almost always a missing or mismatched tag. The error message usually has a line number. If you can't find it, paste the starter again and re-edit more carefully.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Preview didn't update</title>
            <p>
              Click <term>Rebuild</term> or press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd>. The preview doesn't refresh on its own.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Lost your work</title>
            <p>
              Click <term>Projects</term> in the top nav. It's saved.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Something weirder</title>
            <p>
              Raise your hand. Or ask the person next to you first — chances are they've just hit the same thing.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- 5:35-5:40 Share with a Neighbor                    -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-share-lesson">
    <title>5:35-5:40 Share with a Neighbor</title>

    <p>
      Before we regroup, trade lessons with the person next to you. Five minutes, two minutes each way.
    </p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>How to share your link</title>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Save your project (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd>) and click <term>Back to projects</term>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Click your project, then <term>Sharable link</term>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Copy the URL and give it to your neighbor — on paper, by email, or just turn your screen.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>What to look for</title>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>What's one thing their lesson does well?</li>
          <li>What's one thing you'd like to know how to do after seeing theirs?</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        That's it. This is a chance to see someone else's work, not a code review.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- 5:40-5:50 Show and Tell                            -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-show-tell">
    <title>5:40-5:50 Show and Tell</title>

    <p>
      We'll look at two or three lessons together on the projector. If you'd like yours shown, let the facilitator know. If you'd rather not, that's fine — there's no pressure.
    </p>

    <p>
      For each one, we'll do the same quick tour: what's the topic, what <pretext/> structures did it use, and what's one thing that was harder than expected.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- 5:50-6:00 Next Steps and Q & A                     -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-next-steps">
    <title>5:50-6:00 Next Steps and Q &amp; A</title>

    <p>
      You now have a working lesson, a shareable link, and the rhythm of <pretext/> authoring. Here are three directions you might take it next.
    </p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Keep using PreTeXt.Plus</title>
      <p>
        Your free account stays. You can create up to ten projects — plenty for a term's worth of handouts, a worksheet series, or a short open-access textbook. If you adopt <pretext/> seriously, this is a perfectly good home for a while.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Move to GitHub Codespaces when you're ready</title>
      <p>
        When you want version control, multi-file projects, custom assets, or to publish to your own <c>github.io</c> URL, the Codespaces workflow gives you all of that with the same browser-based feel. The <em>Codespace Guide</em> chapter in this hub walks through it step by step. It's a take-home — don't worry about it tonight.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Install locally (long-term)</title>
      <p>
        Eventually, some of you will want <pretext/> running on your own machine. When that day comes, start at <url href="https://pretextbook.org"><c>pretextbook.org</c></url>. No rush — the browser workflows will take you very far.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Community</title>
      <p>
        The <pretext/> community is active and welcoming. See the <em>Information &amp; Links</em> chapter in this hub for forums, documentation, and the author's guide.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Questions</title>
      <p>
        Whatever's on your mind — about today, about where to go next, or about whether <pretext/> fits a specific thing you teach. The floor is yours.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- Appendix: Fallback plan                            -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-fallback-plan">
    <title>Appendix — Fallback Plan</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        <em>Facilitator reference.</em> The timeline above assumes the workshop runs as designed — everyone gets online, everyone's <c>pretext.plus</c> account works, and the editor behaves. This appendix is what to do if that isn't true.
      </p>

      <p>
        The goal of the fallback is not to replicate the full hands-on experience. It's to preserve the <em>core takeaway</em>: participants leave understanding what <pretext/> authoring looks and feels like, and with a concrete sense of what they'd write if they tried it themselves.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="fb-triggers">
      <title>When to switch to fallback mode</title>

      <p>
        Switch — fully or partially — if any of these happen:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>The venue Wi-Fi is down or throttled badly enough that <c>pretext.plus</c> won't load for most of the room.</li>
          <li>The <c>pretext.plus</c> service is slow, broken, or rejecting sign-ups.</li>
          <li>An institutional firewall is blocking account confirmation emails.</li>
          <li>More than about a quarter of the room can't get their editor working after ten minutes of troubleshooting.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Don't switch the whole room if only two or three people are stuck — pair them with neighbors who are working and keep going. Switch the whole room if the pattern is clearly environmental.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="fb-levels">
      <title>Graceful degradation — three levels</title>

      <p>
        <dl>
          <li>
            <title>Level 1 — Pair up</title>
            <p>
              <em>Use when:</em> a minority of the room is offline or account-blocked.
            </p>
            <p>
              Ask each stuck participant to pair with a neighbor who has a working editor. The partner drives; the stuck participant reads aloud the snippet to paste and decides what to customize. Everyone still sees the Edit → Rebuild rhythm and leaves with a finished lesson associated with at least one of them.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Level 2 — Instructor-driven demo with paper exercises</title>
            <p>
              <em>Use when:</em> most of the room cannot edit, but the projector still works.
            </p>
            <p>
              The facilitator drives a single <c>pretext.plus</c> project on the projector. Work through the five Guided Practice exercises live, one at a time, with the room calling out small edits. Distribute the printed participant handout so people can annotate each snippet by hand, writing in the changes they'd make. For <em>Build a Lesson</em>, hand out the two starter options on paper and ask participants to mark them up with their own topic — title changes, example swaps, reworded exercises. Collect a few at the end and project them as the Show and Tell.
            </p>
            <p>
              This level still preserves the core: participants see source, see preview, see a cross-reference resolve, and produce something concrete.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Level 3 — Fully offline read-and-compare</title>
            <p>
              <em>Use when:</em> nothing online is usable, not even for the facilitator.
            </p>
            <p>
              Distribute the prebuilt offline packet (see the next subsection). Walk through the <q>source on the left, output on the right</q> comparison sheets, then have participants edit a printed starter lesson in pencil and share with a neighbor. The session still ends on time and participants leave with a realistic mental model of the workflow.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="fb-offline-packet">
      <title>Offline packet to have on hand</title>

      <p>
        Bring the following in printed form (or on a local USB drive) before the session starts. This is cheap insurance.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <em>Screenshots of the editor.</em> The <c>pretext.plus</c> landing page, an empty project, a project with source on the left and preview on the right, and the <term>Sharable link</term> dialog. Enough for someone who has never seen the tool to recognize what's happening.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <em>Side-by-side <q>source vs. output</q> sheets</em> for each of the five Guided Practice exercises. Left column: the <pretext/> snippet. Right column: a screenshot of the rendered result.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <em>Printed starter lessons.</em> Both Build-a-Lesson starters, printed with generous margins so participants can annotate in pencil.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <em>A prebuilt sample HTML output</em> on a USB drive or local file that the facilitator can open without internet, so there's always something real to show on the projector.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <em>This hub document as a PDF</em>, so participants can follow the timeline even without network access.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="fb-completion-path">
      <title>A non-deployment completion path</title>

      <p>
        The default <q>done</q> signal is a sharable link. If sharing is broken, substitute any of these:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>A screenshot of the participant's preview panel, sent to the facilitator or a neighbor.</li>
          <li>A printed marked-up starter lesson handed in at the end.</li>
          <li>A read-aloud of one thing they'd add if they had another hour — a lightweight oral <q>Show and Tell.</q></li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Whatever form it takes, each participant should leave having produced <em>something concrete</em> they could point at and say <q>I made that.</q> That's the contract.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="fb-pacing">
      <title>Pacing adjustments</title>

      <p>
        If you've switched to Level 2 or 3, the timeline still holds roughly, but cut the following first:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Interactive Feature Gallery (4:10-4:20)</em> — compress to three favorites, shown on projector from the prebuilt HTML.</li>
          <li><em>Finished-early prompts in Guided Practice</em> — drop entirely.</li>
          <li><em>Show and Tell (5:40-5:50)</em> — shorten to one lesson or one collected paper starter, and give the extra minutes back to Build-a-Lesson.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Protect the Build-a-Lesson phase. That's where the core learning happens, and it's the last thing to cut.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

</chapter>
